auth {
  mode: none
}

headers {
  Accept: application/json
  ~X-MINERVA-REQUEST-ID: 3f1c9f9e-6f2a-4c2b-9a1e-2f0d5f7c8b31
}

docs {
  # @minerva/api

  Every request in this collection targets `apps/backend/api` — the NestJS service that the
  frontends call. The health routes come from `src/health/controller/health.controller.ts`
  and the job route from `src/app.controller.ts`; the `EmailJob` payload comes from
  `@minerva/typescript-lib` (`shared/typescript-lib/src/queue.ts`). The same routes are
  browsable as Swagger UI at `{{baseUrl}}/docs`.

  Select the **local** environment before sending anything — `{{baseUrl}}` is defined
  there, not here.

  `Accept: application/json` below covers every JSON route. `GET /health/ping` answers
  `text/plain` and overrides it on the request itself.

  `X-MINERVA-REQUEST-ID` sits here disabled because *every* route accepts it, not just the
  ones that mention it — `RequestIdMiddleware` runs in front of the whole app, so the
  header is a collection-wide concern in exactly the way `Accept` is. Send a uuid v4 and
  the api answers under it; send nothing, or something that is not a uuid v4, and it mints
  one. Either way it comes back in the response header of every route, which is why the
  requests below assert it is there. Switch it on to name a request before making it —
  but switch it off again before sending two, or both get answered under the same id.
  OpenAPI knows about it as a global parameter, so `{{baseUrl}}/docs` shows it on every
  operation too.

  The service has no authentication yet, so the collection-level auth mode is `none` and
  every request inherits it. When auth arrives, set it once here rather than per request.
}
